 "decisions": [
  {
   "id": "ADR-1",
   "title": "Harden SQLite with a connect listener, connect_args timeout and QueuePool settings",
   "decision": "For file-based SQLite:\n- create_engine(url, poolclass=QueuePool, pool_size, max_overflow, pool_timeout, connect_args={'check_same_thread': False, 'timeout': (SQLITE_BUSY_TIMEOUT_MS + 0.5) / 1000.0}).\n- An event.listens_for(engine, 'connect') handler executes the literal statements 'PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL' and 'PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL' on a raw DBAPI cursor.\n- The +0.5 ms makes pysqlite's int(timeout*1000) truncation land exactly on the configured value.\n\nIn-memory SQLite gets only check_same_thread=False. Non-SQLite gets pool_pre_ping=True plus the pool settings.",
   "rationale": "WAL lets readers proceed while a writer holds the lock, which is what stops redirects queueing behind creates and clicks. The sqlite3 timeout parameter installs the busy handler without building a PRAGMA string, so the literal-only SQL policy holds. QueuePool kwargs are not valid for SingletonThreadPool/StaticPool, so in-memory URLs skip them.",
   "alternatives": [
    "PRAGMA busy_timeout=<n> built with formatting (violates policy)",
    "Move to PostgreSQL now (rejected by Q-2; needs a driver)",
    "Leave the rollback journal and only add a busy timeout (readers would still block)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ADR-2",
   "title": "Idempotent startup ALTER instead of migration tooling",
   "decision": "After create_all, the lifespan calls app.core.schema.ensure_links_expires_at(engine):\n- It uses sqlalchemy.inspect on a fresh connection.\n- If 'expires_at' is not among the links columns, it runs the dialect-guarded literal ALTER inside engine.begin().\n- It logs 'added' or 'already_present'.\n- On any exception it logs an ERROR and re-raises, so startup aborts.",
   "rationale": "There is one additive, nullable column and a one-week deadline. Alembic would add a dependency, a workflow and operator steps. Without the upgrade, every redirect on the deployed DB would return 503. Failing startup loudly is better than serving 503s.",
   "alternatives": [
    "Alembic migrations (out of scope)",
    "Documented manual ALTER only (operator error risk RK-11)",
    "A separate link_expiry table (more joins on the hot path)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ADR-3",
   "title": "Expiry evaluated in Python on the narrow lookup row, with an injectable clock",
   "decision": "LinkRepository.get_redirect_target selects only id, url, is_active and expires_at. LinkService._check_redirectable(target) raises GoneError() if the link is inactive, then GoneError(LINK_EXPIRED_DETAIL) if expires_at is not None and self._utcnow() >= expires_at. GET and HEAD both go through _resolve_target, which calls _check_redirectable. The clock is LinkService(now=callable|None); _utcnow() calls it, falling back to models.utcnow(), and normalises to naive UTC.",
   "rationale": "This adds no SQL statements (FR-17). The rule is evaluated on every request with no cache (FR-22), and both methods share one helper (RK-14). Deactivation takes precedence. Tests are deterministic.",
   "alternatives": [
    "WHERE expires_at > :now in SQL (cannot distinguish 404, 410-deactivated and 410-expired without a second query)",
    "In-process cache of links (forbidden; breaks immediate deactivation and expiry)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ADR-4",
   "title": "Backward-compatible idempotency hash",
   "decision": "request_hash = sha256(url) when expires_at is None; otherwise sha256(url + '\\n' + canonical UTC instant 'YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS.ffffffZ'). The replay check runs before the future-instant check.",
   "rationale": "Keys stored before the deploy (url-only hashes) keep replaying. The same instant written with different offsets hashes identically. Adding or changing expires_at gives 409. Replays return the stored link even after the expiry has passed, which matches how deactivated links replay.",
   "alternatives": [
    "Always hash a JSON payload (breaks old keys, RK-15)",
    "Ignore expires_at in the hash (silent replay of a different payload)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ADR-5",
   "title": "SQL aggregation with dialect-guarded bucket expressions that use no bound parameters",
   "decision": "Daily and hourly stats use SELECT bucket, COUNT(id) ... WHERE link_id = :id AND created_at >= :start [AND created_at < :end] GROUP BY bucket.\n\nDay bucket:\n- sqlite: func.date(created_at)\n- postgresql (and the fallback): cast(created_at, Date)\n- mysql/mariadb: func.date(created_at)\n\nHour bucket:\n- sqlite: func.strftime(literal_column(\"'%Y-%m-%d %H:00:00'\"), created_at)\n- postgresql (and the fallback): func.date_trunc(literal_column(\"'hour'\"), created_at)\n- mysql/mariadb: func.timestampadd(literal_column('HOUR'), func.hour(created_at), func.date(created_at))\n\nResult values are normalised in Python (str -> date/datetime). Module-level statement builders take dialect_name so tests can compile them for all three dialects.",
   "rationale": "Memory is O(days) or O(hours) and the query uses the covering (link_id, created_at) index. With no bind parameters inside the bucket expression, the GROUP BY matches the SELECT expression on PostgreSQL. String formats are constants, not SQL formatting.",
   "alternatives": [
    "Stream timestamps with yield_per and bucket in Python (portable but O(clicks) time)",
    "Denormalised hourly counter table (new schema, out of scope)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ADR-6",
   "title": "Explicit HEAD routes for the probes",
   "decision": "health.py adds @router.head('/healthz', include_in_schema=False) and @router.head('/readyz', include_in_schema=False). They return Response(status_code=200) with an empty body. /readyz shares a private _check_database(db) helper with GET.",
   "rationale": "This guarantees an empty body and leaves the OpenAPI path and operation set untouched. The health router is included before the catch-all, so HEAD /{code} never sees these paths. POST still yields 405.",
   "alternatives": [
    "api_route(methods=['GET','HEAD']) (adds 'head' operations to the OpenAPI schema and relies on the server to strip the body)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ADR-7",
   "title": "End the read transaction before the click insert; best-effort WARNING on failure",
   "decision": "In resolve_and_record_click, after the lookup and checks, call self.session.rollback(). This ends the read transaction and is a transaction-boundary call, not a statement. Then call ClickRepository.add(...) and session.commit() inside try/except Exception. On failure:\n- Roll back, suppressing any secondary exception.\n- Log exactly one logger.warning('failed to record click for link_id=%s (%s)', link_id, type(exc).__name__), with no IP, no exception message and no traceback.\n- Return the URL.",
   "rationale": "This avoids SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT on the read-to-write upgrade under WAL and bounds the added latency to the busy timeout. The exception message can contain bound SQL parameters, so it is not logged. ClickRepository.add remains the patch point used by existing tests.",
   "alternatives": [
    "session.commit() after the read (shows up as a second COMMIT in the query budget)",
    "BEGIN IMMEDIATE for the whole redirect (serialises reads behind writers)",
    "Asynchronous click ingestion (out of scope, Q-5)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ADR-8",
   "title": "Future-instant validation in the service with a 422 domain error",
   "decision": "Add InvalidInputError(AppError) with status 422 in app/core/errors.py. Schemas only parse and normalise (to naive UTC, non-string values rejected). LinkService raises InvalidInputError('expires_at must be in the future') using its injectable clock. StatsService raises InvalidInputError for invalid hourly ranges.",
   "rationale": "Validation that depends on the clock must use the injectable clock and must be skipped on idempotent replay. A Pydantic validator cannot do either. The error body stays {\"detail\": \"...\"}.",
   "alternatives": [
    "Pydantic validator calling datetime.now() (cannot be tested deterministically; would break replay)",
    "Raise HTTPException from the services (violates layering)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ADR-9",
   "title": "No-store on redirect-path error responses",
   "decision": "redirect.py catches NotFoundError and GoneError from the service and returns JSONResponse({'detail': exc.detail}, status_code=exc.status_code, headers={'Cache-Control': 'no-store'}). Other exceptions propagate to the global handlers (SQLAlchemyError -> 503).",
   "rationale": "Browsers and CDNs could otherwise cache a 410 or 404 after a link is created, cleared or extended. Expiry and PATCH changes take effect immediately (NFR-15).",
   "alternatives": [
    "Add no-store to every AppError in the global handler (wider blast radius)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ADR-10",
   "title": "MySQL case-sensitive codes via a type variant",
   "decision": "Declare code as String(32).with_variant(String(32, collation='utf8mb4_bin'), 'mysql', 'mariadb'). Document the manual DDL for existing MySQL databases: ALTER TABLE links MODIFY code VARCHAR(32) CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_bin NOT NULL;",
   "rationale": "This keeps portable sqltypes (type(col.type) is still sqltypes.String) and leaves the SQLite and PostgreSQL DDL unchanged.",
   "alternatives": [
    "A global collation (breaks SQLite)",
    "Dialect-specific types from sqlalchemy.dialects (forbidden in models)"
   ]
  }
 ],
